{"author":{"id":"2f242a7e7f2592f8909954a77fabece74f6e8cc197182647ed3b15ea8e3878af"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790979868,"nonce":"vxkGebIMHj/F3QRXeFB0ESZpvmU=","title":"Window.setSize on Wayland changes the size without a Resized event","message":"`window_set_size` calls winit's `request_inner_size` and drops what it returns. On Wayland a floating window's size is applied at once: winit returns `Some(size)` and, as its docs allow, sends no `WindowEvent::Resized`. So the program is never told its window changed size; `width`/`height` report the new size, but a loop that resizes its surface on `Resized` keeps drawing at the old one.\n\nSeen under headless sway 1.9 (wlroots 0.17) with the desktop test: after `setSize(360, 260)` on a floating window, no `Resized` reaches that window within 5 s, on either the window resized or the other. On X11 (Xvfb with openbox) the same call produces `Resized`.\n\nReproduce: `XWINDOW_DESKTOP=1 cargo test -p xwindow-check --test desktop` under sway; the checks \"events go to the window they are for\" and \"closing one window leaves the other\" step around it.\n\nWhen `request_inner_size` returns `Some(size)` that differs from the size last reported, the backend could queue `Event::Resized` with it on that window, as the browser backend posts a canvas size as soon as a command sets it.","files":null}]}